WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:13.150
It’s the world’s largest population, the
second largest economy, has one of the largest

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:17.920
multi-currency reserves, the second largest
military budget, the is the number one trade

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:18.920
power globally…

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:22.119
But it’s better known as: “The world’s
factory”

00:00:22.119 --> 00:00:25.160
These are just a few of the reasons that China
has become a superpower:

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:29.260
Now, if you are a regular viewer of Visual
Politik, you know that we have dedicated several

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:30.910
videos to this country:

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:34.780
We have discussed its foreign policy, its
debt issues, we’ve discussed how its companies

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:40.260
have grown all around the world, as well as
its political system, Macau, Hong Kong…

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:45.550
Suffice to say, we’ve talked a lot about
China...

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:52.200
And we have done so because we believe that,
nowadays, it is impossible…

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.559
as well as pretty inconvenient to discuss
international politics without keeping in

00:00:56.559 --> 00:00:58.809
mind this huge global player.

00:00:58.809 --> 00:01:02.740
However, in all those previous videos you
could say that we have skipped a really important

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:07.340
questions: “How powerful is China today,
really?”

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:10.630
Well, that’s exactly the question that we
are going to answer in this video.

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:15.490
And we’re going to do it from three different
perspectives: military, economic and political.

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:20.470
And look, even though many people today think
of China as second most important global power…

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:23.280
Not everyone agrees with this, not at all
in fact.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.440
Some experts think that, when China’s potential
is carefully examined…well, it’s not actually

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:29.800
that powerful at all.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:34.421
But then again there are plenty of other experts
who state that Chinese power is growing at

00:01:34.421 --> 00:01:39.789
an incredibly fast rate, and that the 21st
Century is most certainly going to be China’s

00:01:39.789 --> 00:01:40.789
century.

00:01:40.789 --> 00:01:44.970
Now, before we get started, we should establish
one thing first: What exactly do we mean by

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:45.970
power?

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:47.870
Is it to have a lot of economic resources?

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:49.060
How about a big army?

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:51.430
Or maybe it’s having many natural resources?

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:56.910
Well, social scientists consider political
power to be found in leverage and influence.

00:01:56.910 --> 00:02:01.690
That is essentially saying that power is the
ability to shape and condition the events

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:03.390
and actions of others.

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:06.150
So… the question now becomes: What is China´s
power?

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:10.789
What power do they have to leverage and influence
other countries?

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:15.430
Well, as I said a moment ago, in this video
we are going to examine the three main areas

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:20.790
that most often delivery that power: weapons,
economics, and politics.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:27.690
And let’s start things off with WAR, shall
we?

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:31.950
(THE DRAGON’S STRENGTH)

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:36.540
It isn't exactly easy to quantify the military
strength of a country.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.240
It depends on several factors:

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:42.710
You must consider how protected the country
is, if its soldiers are well trained or not,

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:46.200
how modern its arsenal is... and a whole bunch
of other factors.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.960
However, despite these different factors,
we can still say the spending on defense is

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:55.490
usually the best way to measure the military
potential of a nation.

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:59.490
Further, when we analyze the the money a country
spends on its army we also get some other

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:06.460
relevant information, like how important a
strong military is for the government.

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:13.690
So, according to the official figures, in
2016, China spent around $146 billion on defense.

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:18.510
But as you can imagine, the Chinese government
is not the best example of transparency, and

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:23.570
some other sources indicated that the number
could be closer to $226 billion.

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:28.330
Anyway, whichever number is correct, we do
know that China has multiplied its defense

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:33.709
spending by five in the last decade and it
is today the second largest spending country

00:03:33.709 --> 00:03:39.819
in the world when it comes to defense spending,
trailing only behind the United States.

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:45.569
5 times is a significant increase in such
a short period of time.

00:03:45.569 --> 00:03:49.350
However, this hasn’t been disproportional
compared to the country's GDP.

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:53.980
Indeed, spending has been closely related
to the economic growth of the country, having

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:57.020
been kept within 2% of the overall GDP.

00:03:57.020 --> 00:03:59.060
And that is not the end of it.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:03.240
If we look at the public budget, we can see
how defense expenses have dropped from 12%

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:08.790
of the public spending in 2001, to around
a little more than 6% in 2016.

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:11.240
And, can you guess what that means?

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:15.461
It means that China has made less of an effort
(when it comes to spending) than the US, when

00:04:15.461 --> 00:04:19.840
you consider GDP and public spending as reference
points.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:30.300
Take a look at this chart, for instance:
We already know two things:

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:34.440
First, that the Chinese military spending
will keep growing at the same level the economy

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.410
does, which will gradually shrink that difference
with the US;

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:41.790
Secondly, we know that military spending is
only a small burden to the Chinese economy…

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:45.591
We could say it is a sustainable amount and
if the government needed to reduce it, the

00:04:45.591 --> 00:04:48.380
government could manage to do so without too
many problems.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:52.630
However, it’s now important that we talk
about military capability, because when it

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:56.840
comes to Chinese military power there are
certain…

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:58.200
limitations…

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:02.690
(WHAT DOES THE DRAGON LACK?)

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:07.880
China´s potential to operate military force
outside of their borders is actually quite

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:08.880
low.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.220
Unlike the United States, China does not have
any military bases abroad (with the exception

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:15.150
of Djibouti as we will see later on).

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:20.130
It also doesn’t have that many allies either,
or a logistic network, or satellites for large

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:22.870
deployments...

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:25.060
And then you must consider their lack of experience.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:30.000
It’s been forty years since the last time
Chinese troops engaged in combat.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.740
But, does that mean China is some sort of
“paper tiger”?

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.880
Well, no, absolutely not.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:41.550
New resources are allowing Beijing to make
important changes and investments:

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:45.911
“China’s growing military potential has
changed the balance of forces in Asia, and

00:05:45.911 --> 00:05:50.190
the People’s Liberation Army is now capable
of conducting operations on a global scale,”

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:51.190
Valery Kashin

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:52.300
China is making changes.

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:57.850
They have reduced their military by 300,000
soldiers since 2015 and have focused on military

00:05:57.850 --> 00:06:00.270
upgrades and their ability to operate abroad.

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:04.900
The Chinese army opened its first foreign
military base in July 2017, in the African

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:06.160
country of Djibouti.

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:12.060
In 2012, China bought its first aircraft carrier
from Ukraine and, in 2017, they completed

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:14.540
their first domestic carrier with more to
follow.

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:18.630
It is also investing in missiles and its air
force.

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:23.210
“China has put into service its new generation
J-20 stealth fighter”

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:28.510
Yes, it will take decades, at a minimum, for
the Chinese army to come close to the American

00:06:28.510 --> 00:06:33.430
army but there is certainly no other country
out their with as much potential as China.

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:38.210
And we all better get used to having to deal
with China when talking about global military

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:39.210
strategy...

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:46.120
(ECONOMIC POWER)

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:50.350
There is no way to sustain any political or
military power without economic capacity.

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:55.690
But there’s more to it than that: economic
power has its own great potential to influence

00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:57.900
other governments and large companies.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:01.200
And this is what is happening, for instance,
with direct foreign investment - which is

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:03.380
both entering and leaving China.

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:07.400
As an pure financial figure amount, this is
not actually very high just yet, but year

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:10.070
after year, this figure has grown and grown…

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:14.540
On a previous VisualPolitik video we looked
at how, in the last few years, Chinese companies

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:18.470
have started to invest money around the world.

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:28.370
In 2016 (excluding Hong Kong and Macau) this
amount was more than $180 billion.

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:31.770
Thanks to this, China has gained influence
especially in developing countries that have

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:36.540
seen quite the opportunity in this Chinese
money.

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:45.580
And it’s the same sort of thing with trade,
but here China is, by far the leading global

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:46.580
power.

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:50.340
And we’re not just talking about Chinese
exports here - it imports a lot too.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:54.229
Pay attention here because Chinese economic
growth has created a more prosperous middle

00:07:54.229 --> 00:07:55.229
class.

00:07:55.229 --> 00:08:01.150
Every day, more than 180,000 people leave
poverty behind and become part of the middle

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:03.210
class that has enough money to…well, spend.

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:07.930
And this, dear friends, is something that
has caused many multinationals to make China

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:11.199
their number one priority.

00:08:11.199 --> 00:08:16.120
That is certainly the case at BMW where one
out of every four cars sold by this company

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:21.270
is sold in China - a country where sales are
now 50% more than the US.

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:25.610
But there’s more, Asian assembly lines occupy
several countries.

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:30.440
For example, Vietnam may produce one component,
while another is made in South Korea, and

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:35.070
later they are all assembled in China, the
world’s great production centre.

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:41.539
In the Asia-Pacific region, hundreds of thousands
of jobs rely on trade with China...

00:08:41.539 --> 00:08:46.929
OK, but not everything looks so good, there
are problems too, especially when it comes

00:08:46.929 --> 00:08:49.839
to technology, design and innovation…

00:08:49.839 --> 00:08:55.429
Because, truth be told, innovation and creativity
don't usually get on with dictatorships.

00:08:55.429 --> 00:09:00.420
But still, things are changing, and investment,
trade and multinationals are all things that

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:07.230
generate a lot of influence indeed.

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:16.170
(A GROWING POLITICAL POWER)

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:17.449
Finally there is political power.

00:09:17.449 --> 00:09:22.160
It is often said China lacks leadership and
further that, unlike the US (which counts

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:26.220
on solid allies all around the world), China
is some sort of lone wolf.

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:31.589
Now, this has been said for years but do keep
it in mind that this is all beginning to change.

00:09:31.589 --> 00:09:36.339
China has bet on increasing their influence
on global politics and is doing that to defend

00:09:36.339 --> 00:09:39.470
interests that are increasingly global.

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:46.029
It is a clear strategy: the Chinese are using
their large currency reserves to expand their

00:09:46.029 --> 00:09:47.029
influence.

00:09:47.029 --> 00:09:51.179
In the last decade alone, Beijing has loaned
money to several governments and has invested

00:09:51.179 --> 00:09:53.420
all over the world, from Fiji to Madagascar.

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:58.999
And that is not the end of it, they have also
set up ambitious plans to achieve their goals.

00:09:58.999 --> 00:10:04.389
As an example, the Asian Infrastructure Investment
Bank, a financial power controlled by China

00:10:04.389 --> 00:10:09.379
and based in Beijing, will reduce the influence
of institutions like the World Bank, the IMF

00:10:09.379 --> 00:10:14.100
or the Asian Development Bank - all of them
controlled by Washington to some extent.

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:15.970
And how about some more examples?

00:10:15.970 --> 00:10:19.999
Well there is the New Silk Road, the One c
Road project and One Belt, the largest infrastructure

00:10:19.999 --> 00:10:21.009
project in the world...

00:10:21.009 --> 00:10:26.360
Then there is the China Investment Corporation,
a sovereign fund that manages more than $800

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:29.809
billion and invests in companies all around
the world.

00:10:29.809 --> 00:10:32.420
Yep, that’s billion with a “B”.

00:10:32.420 --> 00:10:33.769
But…

00:10:33.769 --> 00:10:39.050
now you might be wondering… has China actually
had any political success with this strategy?

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:43.490
Well, yes, they have, and that is well demonstrated
in Greece.

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:48.529
China, dear friends, is developing enormous
projects in this country, like the Piraeus

00:10:48.529 --> 00:10:53.509
Port or the Hellinikon, a sort of Monaco in
the heart of Athens.

00:10:53.509 --> 00:10:58.389
And what’s the result of all this?

00:10:58.389 --> 00:11:04.009
Well, last June, Athens vetoed an EU resolution
that condemned the violation of rights in

00:11:04.009 --> 00:11:05.009
China…

00:11:05.009 --> 00:11:06.009
And it’s not the first time.

00:11:06.009 --> 00:11:10.990
Anyway, China is facing real challenges: the
ageing of its population perhaps being the

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:11.990
most important one.

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:15.029
Just have a look at this chart:

00:11:15.029 --> 00:11:21.269
But, despite this, the influence of China
in international politics will grow quite

00:11:21.269 --> 00:11:26.929
a lot in the next few years, and China is
already a new superpower.

00:11:26.929 --> 00:11:28.170
And now it is your turn.

00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:31.389
Do you think China is a superpower or just
a flash in the pan?

00:11:31.389 --> 00:11:36.149
How do you think China should be included
in the international community?

00:11:36.149 --> 00:11:39.550
As always, you can leave your answer in the
comments or in this survey.

00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:42.899
Also, do hit that like button if you enjoyed
this video, and don’t forget to subscribe

00:11:42.899 --> 00:11:45.350
for new videos every Monday and Thursday.

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:50.509
Further, check out the Reconsider Media Podcast
- they provided the vocals in this video that

00:11:50.509 --> 00:11:51.509
weren’t mine.

00:11:51.509 --> 00:11:53.190
And as always, thank you for watching.

